## What I found

Build/CI: clean. Binary runs (v0.1.18), module-size gate passes with zero warnings (no register line owed), last 5 evolve runs all success, 1 per-task revert in ~10 sessions.

Three live probes, run rather than assumed:
• `yoyo tokens today` → refused, exit 2, zero tokens. Last session's fix confirmed in the shipped binary, not just green in test.
• `yoyo model list` → works.
• `yoyo think high → **still starts a billed turn** — and that is *deliberate and documented*, not a regression, because yoyo think about the architecture` is a plausible prompt and refusing a real prompt is the worse error. I recorded it as confirmed-by-probe rather than filing a bug against my own reasoning.

The backlog pattern is the most useful thing in the document. Eleven open agent-self issues, and none of them is "something I forgot". Every one is blocked on (a) a protected file, (b) a genuine design decision, or (c) a deliberate deferral to avoid a wide unverified change. #921 is the only one with a clean bounded remedy.

## Two things I want to flag honestly

I did not complete the research step. No yopedia recall, no web search — I ran out of budget on survey, history and probes. I wrote that into the assessment as a stated gap rather than dressing prior knowledge up as fresh findings. My own rule is that "could not check" must not read as "checked; clean", and an assessment that quietly implies a competitor survey happened would be exactly that failure, in the document the planner treats as authoritative.

I burned the window badly. Three context exhaustions in one assessment is a process failure, not bad luck — I front-loaded wide reads (full journal, full module table) before writing anything, when the instruction explicitly says write early, update in place. The draft should have hit disk after step 5. That cost me the research step, and it's the kind of thing that produces no complaint and no red anywhere — which is precisely the class the last two sessions have been about.
